speechtext:
This is the bill on which
we had a brief discussion the other evening and as regards which we decided to ask for a conference with the Senate. I, therefore, beg to move:
That a message be sent to the Senate to acquaint Their Honours that this House disagrees to their amendment to Bill No 117, to amend the Fruit Act, foi the following reason;
Because, (a) It refers to an association by a wrong title
(b)	The holding of consultations with an association whose membership is recruited from all points in Canada may often be very difficult and even impossible ;
(c)	The mere fact of consulting such a body would not bring actual results and seems an unnecessary condition.

We found it in bad shape under my right bon. friend's management and it has taken some time to get it straightened out. Everyone will agree to that. The Maritime -provinces, I say, consider themselves in a unique position, and they are. They contend that under the terms of confederation they were to be given, in compensation for sacrificing the market to the south, access to the central markets of Canada, and a railway was to be built for that purpose and not with any idea of profit in view. And they have always contended, with some force, that this railway, so far as they themselves are concerned, should -be run for the benefit of the people of the Maritime provinces, largely to cany out the object conceived and enunciated at confederation, that they should be enabled to place their products in the markets of old Canada-, as this part of the Dominion was then called, seeing that they had given up their markets across the line. If that is the case, they are entitled to the fullest consideration. But it will be argued that the road is being run at a loss. I am not going to discuss that question, but I do say that the contention of the Maritime provinces, now that we are endeavouring to clear up the rate situation with a vifew to placing all parts of the country on a basis of equality, ought to be, as it will be, fully considered. And the order in council refers to that situation.
In 1919 certain rates were Taised very materially, and to this increase the Maritime provinces seriously objected. The Governor in Council, very wisely as I think, understanding something of the situation, calls the attention of the Board of Railway Commissioners to the feeling in that part of the country and asks that it be given consideration when the board comes to -make its rate structure. I will not dilate further on this matter, but what the government has in view, and all that it has in view, is to bring about in Canada a better feeling among the different sections of the country in relation to this question of transportation. Now, freight rates in Canada are high, but they are lower in this country than they are to the south of us;

and we can never expect as low rates here as we had years ago, because the cost of transportation will never come down sufficiently to allow them to be reduced to the point where they were fifteen years ago. But there is a basis of equality that may be found, and we hope that under this legislation, in which the Board of Railway Commissioners, with the restrictions to which I have referred, is given a free hand throughout the Dominion, a better condition will result from the reconstruction of the rate fabric, so that there will be in the future a greater degree of contentment on the part of all the provinces of Canada, who will feel that they have been given a fair show in the fixing -of rates for the carrying of their products.
